WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.503
Renting a GPU provides temporary access to accelerator hardware without purchasing, installing,

00:00:04.503 --> 00:00:05.971
powering, and maintaining it.

00:00:05.971 --> 00:00:10.740
A user can provision a remote machine, run a model or data job,

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:13.309
collect the results, and release the resource.

00:00:13.309 --> 00:00:16.611
This is valuable when the requirement is short-lived, uncertain,

00:00:16.611 --> 00:00:20.280
or beyond the memory and performance of available local systems.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:23.949
Common reasons include evaluating a larger model before buying hardware,

00:00:23.949 --> 00:00:27.985
completing a temporary fine-tuning or batch-processing job, absorbing a demand spike,

00:00:27.985 --> 00:00:31.654
and giving a distributed team access to a consistent environment.

00:00:31.654 --> 00:00:35.323
Rental also converts a large capital purchase into operational spending.

00:00:35.323 --> 00:00:39.726
That flexibility is useful when utilization would be too low to justify

00:00:39.726 --> 00:00:43.762
ownership or when the required GPU type may change between projects.

00:00:43.762 --> 00:00:45.963
The trade is not free convenience.

00:00:45.963 --> 00:00:50.366
The renter gives up some physical control and accepts provider capacity limits,

00:00:50.366 --> 00:00:54.402
network dependence, ongoing metered charges, and responsibility for cloud identity, access,

00:00:54.402 --> 00:00:55.503
and data handling.

00:00:55.503 --> 00:00:56.971
Setup time is billable.

00:00:56.971 --> 00:01:01.007
An idle instance can cost as much as a productive one.

00:01:01.007 --> 00:01:04.309
Uploading sensitive prompts or documents changes the data boundary,

00:01:04.309 --> 00:01:07.978
and persistent disks or snapshots can outlive the compute session.

00:01:07.978 --> 00:01:12.748
Begin with a defined job: model, runtime, memory need, expected duration, data classification,

00:01:12.748 --> 00:01:13.848
and acceptable interruption.

00:01:13.848 --> 00:01:17.884
Compare the complete rental cost with local capacity and managed alternatives.

00:01:17.884 --> 00:01:22.287
Renting succeeds when it delivers the needed compute for a controlled period

00:01:22.287 --> 00:01:25.956
and the environment is shut down or deleted deliberately afterward.

00:01:25.956 --> 00:01:29.625
It fails when provisioning is easy enough that ownership, cost,

00:01:29.625 --> 00:01:31.460
and residual data become invisible.
